&nbsp;

040125, JunkerIn:

<b>Diatribe - fair warning.</b> I'll begin with my thesis so you can skip out of here quickly. I think words or text for sale is an inherent conflict of interest and thus should be boycotted. Fiction may be an exception, but even then problems surface.

The thesis is even even more of a problem in lib/ancap circles and that in two ways. The first is the problem of the backlash of the opinion makers, the popular writers. Some writers have thrown all to the wind in order to be moral relative to the state and their only chosen income left is that from writing, that from their valid way of fighting for freedom. They accuse this thesis of wanting to take the food out of the mouths of their children, sabotage the liberty fight, and bring ruin upon critical thinking. Not so. The theoretical immorality here is small in relation to all the others plaguing society and small in comparison to the need to spread the word. They can and should keep on writing and fighting. No problem. I hope though that they keep this idea in mind.

The second concerns the nature of the freedom fight. Much of the fight is seen as moral and thus tends to be emotionally charged and also causes effects, problems, that many are frightened by, many times rightly so&#151;tax evasion, concealed carry, and so on can cause extreme consequenses. That means that many are looking for that silver bullet, sometimes theoretically and sometimes desperately (the police are expected any day now). The reaction to this, in a society with a great many different types, is the attraction of con-artists, like sharks coming to blood. Many are going to see they can write and sell books promising a variety of silver bullets. Beside books and magazine articles, any of a number of other scams have been offered and sold. You can find a great number of them debunked on the internet ([http://www.scamdog.com Scamdog], for instance). And all of this is done to the cries of real people in real jail cells.

So, before I even get to the thesis, remember: Let the children eat and watch closely for scams.

The thesis. The direct purpose of words is communication (no matter the form&#151;speaking or writing). Not only that, most of the time it is communication of information. Sometimes it <i>is</i> to communicate emotion. There's no issue with that here. Emotional art is wonderful. It's the information that's important in this context. In that sense, the purpose of the words is to communicate information (almost all of the time). And to add more to the defense, the issue is also not with the spry turn of phrase or the entertaining anecdote along the way. The issue is the attraction of the sharks where the selling and profit become more important than the information.

There are two purposes here. The one is to use words to inform people. The other is to make money. This is called an inherent conflict of interest. Any conflict of interest is a warning sign. The warning is to get people to look closely at the situation and make sure that the desired side wins out. And with that comes part of the problem&#151;how can one look closely enough at the book before one buys it?

My solution? I've stopped buying books by living authors I don't already know. If I can't get the ideas or advice for free on the internet, I pass. That may be radical, but most of my lib/ancap life is. I also find that there are a lot more cruddy books on the market than cruddy books on the internet. And that's why as well I like the various 'free books on the internet' links up above. I can read Ludwig von Mises. I can read Bram Stoker's Dracula. And all without the problem of a conflict of interest.

And finally, I can also read much by Clair Wolfe, by Wendy <nowiki>McElroy</nowiki>, or by any of a number of other lib/ancap writers. And for them I know on which side of the conflict they come down&#151;hard on the side of truth and freedom. In their case, I've looked closely enough. But, there are many others that I don't know. And I've stopped buying to find out. If I don't know them well from the net, then no buying. That may not seem fair to some new, hard-working writers and I suppose it might look that way. But it is more than fair to many who just want to sell, who must 'publish or perish', or who say they have the silver bullet for not paying taxes.

When you've looked closely, you've done good. And that's enough.

&nbsp;

OnLineBooksTalk
