 ######fit become more important than the information.

There are two purposes here. The one is to use words to inform people. The other is to make money. This is called an inherent conflict of interest. Any conflict of interest is a warning sign. The warning is to get people to look closely at the situation and make sure that the desired side wins out. And with that comes part of the problem&amp;amp;#151;how can one look closely enough at the book before one buys it?

My solution? I&amp;#39;ve stopped buying books by living authors I don&amp;#39;t already know. If I can&amp;#39;t get the ideas or advice for free on the internet, I pass. That may be radical, but most of my lib/ancap life is. I also find that there are a lot more cruddy books on the market than cruddy books on the internet. And that&amp;#39;s why as well I like the various &amp;#39;free books on the internet&amp;#39; links up above. I can read Ludwig von Mises. I can read Bram Stoker&amp;#39;s Dracula. And all without the problem of a conflict of interest.

And finally, I can also read much by Clair Wolfe, by Wendy &amp;lt;nowiki&amp;gt;McElroy&amp;lt;/nowiki&amp;gt;, or by any of a number of other lib/ancap writers. And for them I know on which side of the conflict they come down&amp;amp;#151;hard on the side of truth and freedom. In their case, I&amp;#39;ve looked closely enough. But, there are many others that I don&amp;#39;t know. And I&amp;#39;ve stopped buying to find out. If I don&amp;#39;t know them well from the net, then no buying. That may not seem fair to some new, hard-working writers and I suppose it might look that way. But it is more than fair to many who just want to sell, who must &amp;#39;publish or perish&amp;#39;, or who say they have the silver bullet for not paying taxes.

When you&amp;#39;ve looked closely, you&amp;#39;ve done good. And that&amp;#39;s enough.

&amp;amp;nbsp;

OnLineBooksTalk
